t-comparison trees compare two contracts (or two versions) for key differences, similarities, and a recommendation. The proven shape: :parallel per-contract structured-extraction (:output-schemas guaranteeing typed provisions), then sequential synthesis :llm consuming the structured vectors (NOT raw text). Always extract first, then diff — feeding both raw contracts to a single synthesis :llm hits attention limits and induces hallucinated provisions.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - compares two contracts (or two versions of the same contract) for key differences and similarities\n  - produces :key-differences (provision-level diff), :similarities (shared provisions), :recommendation (which version to prefer or what to negotiate)\n  - two-input shape: separate :contract-a and :contract-b blackboard keys, not a single :document\n  - natural fit for :parallel extraction across both contracts followed by sequential diff synthesis\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** parallel per-contract extraction reduces wall-time when both contracts can be processed independently (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: both contracts share a roughly comparable structure AND the per-contract extraction is identical work\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:contract-a] :writes [:provisions-a] :output-schemas {:provisions-a [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}] [:llm {:reads [:contract-b] :writes [:provisions-b] :output-schemas {:provisions-b [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}]] [:llm {:reads [:provisions-a :provisions-b] :writes [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** diff-synthesis :llm sees TWO large input contexts simultaneously — hits attention/budget limits sooner than single-doc summarization (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: both contracts are >5KB AND the synthesis :llm receives the full text of both directly\n    - Recommended fix: extract structured per-contract provisions first (compact representations), then feed those CONDENSED structures to the synthesis :llm instead of raw text\n\n  - **Failure mode:** without per-contract structured extraction, the synthesis :llm hallucinates provisions that don't exist in either contract (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the synthesis :llm reads raw :contract-a + :contract-b directly without intermediate structured extraction\n    - Recommended fix: always have an intermediate :llm with :output-schemas that extracts a typed provisions vector PER contract before the diff step — the diff :llm consumes the structured vectors, not raw text\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - contract version-vs-version diff (e.g. provider contract v2.0 vs v3.1 — produces provision-level differences + recommendation on which to prefer)\n  - vendor-contract negotiation review (compare current draft vs prior version)\n  - any two-document structural comparison where provision-level diff matters\n\n#### Alternative #1 — Contract-comparison (confidence: 0.98)\nWhy this fits: Identical content to the top-ranked tree-class, providing the same optimized parallel-extraction-then-diff pattern required for comparing two versions of a SaaS contract.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nContract-comparison trees compare two contracts (or two versions) for key differences, similarities, and a recommendation. The proven shape: :parallel per-contract structured-extraction (:output-schemas guaranteeing typed provisions), then sequential synthesis :llm consuming the structured vectors (NOT raw text). Always extract first, then diff — feeding both raw contracts to a single synthesis :llm hits attention limits and induces hallucinated provisions.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - compares two contracts (or two versions of the same contract) for key differences and similarities\n  - produces :key-differences (provision-level diff), :similarities (shared provisions), :recommendation (which version to prefer or what to negotiate)\n  - two-input shape: separate :contract-a and :contract-b blackboard keys, not a single :document\n  - natural fit for :parallel extraction across both contracts followed by sequential diff synthesis\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** parallel per-contract extraction reduces wall-time when both contracts can be processed independently (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: both contracts share a roughly comparable structure AND the per-contract extraction is identical work\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:contract-a] :writes [:provisions-a] :output-schemas {:provisions-a [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}] [:llm {:reads [:contract-b] :writes [:provisions-b] :output-schemas {:provisions-b [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}]] [:llm {:reads [:provisions-a :provisions-b] :writes [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** diff-synthesis :llm sees TWO large input contexts simultaneously — hits attention/budget limits sooner than single-doc summarization (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: both contracts are >5KB AND the synthesis :llm receives the full text of both directly\n    - Recommended fix: extract structured per-contract provisions first (compact representations), then feed those CONDENSED structures to the synthesis :llm instead of raw text\n\n  - **Failure mode:** without per-contract structured extraction, the synthesis :llm hallucinates provisions that don't exist in either contract (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the synthesis :llm reads raw :contract-a + :contract-b directly without intermediate structured extraction\n    - Recommended fix: always have an intermediate :llm with :output-schemas that extracts a typed provisions vector PER contract before the diff step — the diff :llm consumes the structured vectors, not raw text\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - contract version-vs-version diff (e.g. provider contract v2.0 vs v3.1 — produces provision-level differences + recommendation on which to prefer)\n  - vendor-contract negotiation review (compare current draft vs prior version)\n  - any two-document structural comparison where provision-level diff matters\n\n#### Alternative #2 — Comparative summary (confidence: 0.80)\nWhy this fits: While this handles multi-source comparisons, its content specifically recommends 'contract-comparison' when the structure is 'exactly diff-two-versions'. It remains a strong fallback but is structurally more general than the specific contract-comparison class.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nComparative summary is the multi-source research-then-synthesize variant: parallel per-source extraction → :code aggregator structures extractions per source → final :llm synthesizes a structured comparison (common / differences / recommendation). Always use :parallel (not :sequence) for independent per-source stages — wall-time becomes max(per-source). CRITICAL: use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source stages so comparable fields stay aligned. Limit to ~5 sources per synthesis; for more, do hierarchical comparison. Use contract-comparison (parallel-independent task-class) when the structure is exactly diff-two-versions; comparative-summary is for general N-source comparison.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence of :parallel (or :map-each over sources) per-source :llm extraction → :code aggregator → :llm synthesizer that produces a structured comparison\n  - natural for any 'read N versions/sources and produce a comparison' work — product comparison, contract diff, multi-source fact verification\n  - outputs are typically a structured comparison object (per-source key, common-elements, differences, recommendation)\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** the synthesis stage's :reads include ALL per-source extractions structured as a per-source map — supports apples-to-apples comparison by source rather than free-form prose (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the sources are roughly comparable AND a structured comparison is the value (vs free-form narrative)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]] [:code {:reads [:extract-a :extract-b] :writes [:by-source] :fn (fn [{:keys [extract-a extract-b]}] {:by-source {:a extract-a :b extract-b}})}] [:llm {:reads [:by-source] :writes [:common :differences :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:common :differences :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :parallel (NOT :sequence) for the per-source extraction stages when sources are independent — extraction wall-time becomes max(per-source) not sum(per-source) (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: sources are independent (no need to read source B before extracting source A)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** free-form per-source extraction (different :output-schemas per source or open-ended free text) makes the synthesizer's apples-to-apples comparison hard — comparable fields drift across sources (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: per-source extractions use different schemas OR free-form text\n    - Recommended fix: always use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source extraction stages — comparable fields stay aligned, synthesizer can do direct per-field diff\n\n  - **Failure mode:** synthesizing more than ~5 sources in a single :llm call exceeds context — the comparison loses fidelity on per-source detail (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: source count >5 AND each source's extraction is non-trivial\n    - Recommended fix: for >5 sources, hierarchical comparison: cluster sources into 2-3 groups → comparative-summary within each group → final cross-group comparison\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - product-feature comparison across 3-4 vendor docs: per-vendor extraction → side-by-side feature matrix\n  - contract diff across 2 versions: per-version extraction → key-differences + similarities + recommendation\n  - multi-source fact verification: per-source claim extraction → common claims + contradictions + confidence\n\n#### Alternative #3 — Comparative summary (confidence: 0.80)\nWhy this fits: Mirror of the comparative-summary tree-class; it is fit for the shape of comparison but less specialized than the contract-specific fingerprints.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nComparative summary is the multi-source research-then-synthesize variant: parallel per-source extraction → :code aggregator structures extractions per source → final :llm synthesizes a structured comparison (common / differences / recommendation). Always use :parallel (not :sequence) for independent per-source stages — wall-time becomes max(per-source). CRITICAL: use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source stages so comparable fields stay aligned. Limit to ~5 sources per synthesis; for more, do hierarchical comparison. Use contract-comparison (parallel-independent task-class) when the structure is exactly diff-two-versions; comparative-summary is for general N-source comparison.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence of :parallel (or :map-each over sources) per-source :llm extraction → :code aggregator → :llm synthesizer that produces a structured comparison\n  - natural for any 'read N versions/sources and produce a comparison' work — product comparison, contract diff, multi-source fact verification\n  - outputs are typically a structured comparison object (per-source key, common-elements, differences, recommendation)\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** the synthesis stage's :reads include ALL per-source extractions structured as a per-source map — supports apples-to-apples comparison by source rather than free-form prose (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the sources are roughly comparable AND a structured comparison is the value (vs free-form narrative)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]] [:code {:reads [:extract-a :extract-b] :writes [:by-source] :fn (fn [{:keys [extract-a extract-b]}] {:by-source {:a extract-a :b extract-b}})}] [:llm {:reads [:by-source] :writes [:common :differences :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:common :differences :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :parallel (NOT :sequence) for the per-source extraction stages when sources are independent — extraction wall-time becomes max(per-source) not sum(per-source) (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: sources are independent (no need to read source B before extracting source A)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** free-form per-source extraction (different :output-schemas per source or open-ended free text) makes the synthesizer's apples-to-apples comparison hard — comparable fields drift across sources (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: per-source extractions use different schemas OR free-form text\n    - Recommended fix: always use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source extraction stages — comparable fields stay aligned, synthesizer can do direct per-field diff\n\n  - **Failure mode:** synthesizing more than ~5 sources in a single :llm call exceeds context — the comparison loses fidelity on per-source detail (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: source count >5 AND each source's extraction is non-trivial\n    - Recommended fix: for >5 sources, hierarchical comparison: cluster sources into 2-3 groups → comparative-summary within each group → final cross-group comparison\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - product-feature comparison across 3-4 vendor docs: per-vendor extraction → side-by-side feature matrix\n  - contract diff across 2 versions: per-version extraction → key-differences + similarities + recommendation\n  - multi-source fact verification: per-source claim extraction → common claims + contradictions + confidence\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 2 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Analysis (confidence: 0.95)\n   Why this fits: Analysis is the best fit because the task requires reasoning over two documents to produce structured findings (impact analysis on pricing, liability, etc.) with evidence. The recommended pattern for multi-dimensional analysis using :parallel stages perfectly fits the requirement to produce distinct outputs for :impact, :diffs, and :summary. The trait of tying findings to :affected-items (v2 vs v3 sections) ensures the auditability required for a legal comparison.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Analysis reasons over already-extracted items to produce structured findings (patterns / problems / opportunities) with per-finding evidence. Always tie findings to specific items via :affected-items references for downstream auditability. For multi-dimensional analysis, fan out per-dimension :llm stages via :parallel — each stage gets a tighter focus. NEVER skip the extraction stage: analyzing raw documents conflates extraction and reasoning, degrading both. Composes into comparative-summary (multi-source structured findings), chunked-extraction (per-chunk parallel analysis), and sequential-pipeline (extract → analyze → synthesize). Avoid when the task is summarizing (use synthesis) or generating options (use ideation).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given a vector/map of pre-extracted items, produce a structured set of findings (patterns / problems / opportunities) with per-finding evidence\n  - natural fit as a mid-tree stage between extraction (upstream) and synthesis/critique/code-building (downstream)\n  - outputs are typically a vector of findings, each with :type / :evidence / :confidence / :affected-items\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** tie each finding to the specific extracted item(s) that triggered it via an :affected-items reference field — downstream stages can audit, drill down, or prioritize findings by evidence weight (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: downstream stages need to attribute findings to evidence OR rank findings by support\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:extracts] :writes [:findings] :output-schemas {:findings [:vector [:map [:type :string] [:evidence :string] [:confidence :double] [:affected-items [:vector :string]]]]}}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for multi-dimensional analysis (e.g., risk / opportunity / blocker across the same items), use :parallel one-:llm-per-dimension stages — each stage has a tight focus and can produce richer per-dimension reasoning (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the analysis has 2-4 orthogonal dimensions that benefit from separate prompts\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:extracts] :writes [:risks]}] [:llm {:reads [:extracts] :writes [:opportunities]}] [:llm {:reads [:extracts] :writes [:blockers]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** analyzing raw documents (skipping extraction) forces the analysis :llm to do BOTH extraction and reasoning — quality degrades on both since the model can't optimize attention for either (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the input is raw documents AND the analysis needs to attribute findings to specific items\n    - Recommended fix: two-stage: extract structured items first, then analyze over the extracts; preserves analyzability of findings\n\n  - **Failure mode:** single-pass analysis over a long flat list of items loses signal on per-item nuance — findings cluster around the most prominent items (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: item count is high AND each item deserves per-item attention\n    - Recommended fix: use :map-each per-item analysis with bounded :max-concurrency, then aggregate findings — preserves per-item granularity\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - risk analysis over extracted contract clauses: per-clause risk score + reasoning + mitigation\n  - pattern detection across log events: clusters of related events + frequency + suspected cause\n  - gap analysis over a feature list: required-vs-present + missing-items + priority\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                                     :confidence 0.7\n                                     :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :weaknesses [{:trait \"<what fails AND why>\"\n                                      :avoid-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                      :recommended-alternative \"<concrete fix>\"\n                                      :confidence 0.7\n                                      :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :representative-uses [\"<concrete task this child shipped on>\"]\n                        :avoid-when [\"<concrete anti-context>\"]\n                        :summary \"<2-3 sentences naming the domain + load-bearing trait + when to prefer this over the parent>\"\n                        :version 1\n                        :consolidated-from-event-count 0}\n                       :parent b3597aa7-3957-3734-90c6-531d27c08f67)\n\n   - MINT-ADJACENT — if your task is related-but-DISTINCT (not a child of this behavior), mint an adjacent behavior the same way with `:parent nil` (or under whichever listed reference is the true nearest parent).\n   :strengths/:weaknesses are VECTORS OF MAPS (principle-shaped: :trait + context-guard + concrete recommended action + :confidence + :evidence-count) — a vector of bare strings fails schema validation; the rejection is returned in iteration history for correction.\n\n2. Behavioral: Synthesis (confidence: 0.85)\n   Why this fits: Synthesis is highly relevant for the executive summary and final recommendation portions of the task. The recommended-pattern for long artifacts (scaffolding via a :code-built outline) is ideal for a multi-section report containing a summary, impact analysis, and recommendation. It correctly handles integrating multiple structured inputs (the diffs and impact findings) into a cohesive final artifact.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Synthesis integrates multiple structured inputs into a single cohesive artifact (narrative / report / recommendation). Always provide the synthesizer with inputs structured by source-id (not free-form concat) so attribution stays intact. For long artifacts, scaffold via a :code-built outline that the :llm fills section-by-section — preserves coverage. NEVER synthesize >5 substantive inputs in one call: do hierarchical clustering instead. Declare :output-schemas on the synthesis output when downstream stages (critique / validation) need to verify specific sections. Composes into briefing-generation, comparative-summary, and sequential-pipeline shells. Avoid when the task is ideation (new options) or critique (checking against criteria).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given multiple structured inputs (findings, extracts, source quotes), produce a single integrated artifact — narrative, report, recommendation\n  - natural fit at the tail of a tree where multiple parallel branches converge\n  - outputs are typically a single string (or small structured map) where the structure of the inputs has been collapsed into coherent prose\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** make the synthesizer's :reads contain the per-source extractions structured by source-id rather than a free-form concat — supports apples-to-apples reference and avoids the model losing track of which finding came from where (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: synthesis needs to attribute claims to sources OR balance per-source weight\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:by-source] :writes [:narrative] :output-schemas {:narrative :string}}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for long final artifacts, scaffold the synthesis with a :code node that pre-builds a structured outline from the inputs, then have the :llm fill each section — preserves coverage of all inputs (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final artifact is multi-section AND each section maps to a distinct cluster of inputs\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:code {:reads [:findings] :writes [:outline] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:outline (mapv :type (:findings inputs))})}] [:llm {:reads [:findings :outline] :writes [:narrative]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** synthesizing >5 distinct inputs in a single :llm call exceeds context — the model drops inputs from the middle of the context window silently (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: input count >5 AND each input is substantive\n    - Recommended fix: hierarchical synthesis: cluster inputs into 2-3 groups → per-group :llm synthesis → final :llm integrates the group narratives\n\n  - **Failure mode:** free-form synthesis output (no :output-schemas) makes downstream stages (critique / validation) work on unstructured prose — they can't easily check coverage or shape (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: downstream stages need to verify specific sections are present\n    - Recommended fix: declare a structured :output-schemas on the synthesis (e.g., :map with :summary :recommendations :open-questions) — downstream critique/validation can verify each named field\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - multi-source briefing: extracts from N articles → single executive summary with citations\n  - comparative report: per-vendor extractions → structured comparison + recommendation\n  - investigation conclusion: per-witness extractions → integrated timeline + assessment\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                        